From 896465cdacf271c4fd174cc7b3362a69e6e00226 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mostlys4nny <mostlys4nny@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 15:34:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] bluetooth: restore the HCI socket layer Samsung gutted

Samsung commented out the bodies of the HCI socket functions and left bare
'return 0;' behind -- create, bind, ioctl, getname, release, plus the helpers
they call (hci_sock_gen_cookie, hci_sock_free_cookie, hci_sock_bound_ioctl,
create_monitor_ctrl_open/close). They ship Bluetooth through the Android HAL, so
the kernel socket layer was surplus to them.

The result was not a missing feature but a panic: hci_sock_create returned
success while leaving sock->sk NULL, so the first AF_BLUETOOTH socket hit
BUG_ON(!sk) in bt_sock_reclassify_lock and reset the phone --

    kernel BUG at net/bluetooth/af_bluetooth.c:71!
    PC is at bt_sock_create+0x194/0x1a0 [bluetooth]

which is why this port has carried 'Bluetooth panics the kernel, needs kernel
work' as a known-unfixable for so long.

The file is otherwise line-for-line upstream v4.14.150 -- diffing the two shows
only comment markers, inner /* */ rewritten as // so the outer block would not
terminate early, and the stub returns. hci_sec_filter is upstream BlueZ code,
not a Samsung addition, so there is nothing device-specific to preserve.
Restored verbatim from v4.14.150.

Verified on the device: HCI socket opens and closes cleanly, bluebinder bridges
the HAL to vhci, BlueZ reads the real controller address XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX, and a
scan finds real devices with names, SDP UUIDs and RSSI.
